      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781022623380,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "S\nY\nS\nT\nE\nM\n\nB\nU\nS",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "S\nY\nS\nT\nE\nM\n\nB\nU\nS",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Uca8Phr6pA21dJiqKiMN5",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1537.8141996106945,
      "y": -3863.8679802782044,
      "width": 2139.478271484375,
      "height": 1000,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aZ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1435490276,
      "version": 4441,
      "versionNonce": 1082125532,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781027524668,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "I/O Port:\n-> It is an address used by the CPU to communicate with I/O Device\n-> Each register inside the I/O controller has an address. So I/O Port is an address of one of the registers\n\nThere are two ways the I/O devices are connected to System Bus. These also determine how the I/O devices are addressed.\n1) Memory Mapped I/O\n2) Isolated I/O or I/O mapped I/O\n\nMemory Mapped I/O:\n-> I/O devices use the same address space as the main memory\n-> Each device is assigned one or more memory addresses and CPU communicates with the device by reading from or writing to those memory addresses.\n-> The advantage of this is, we can use same instructions we use for memory with I/O devices as well.\n-> The address decoder is hardwired to I/O devices to access them.\n-> There is no need to define new instructions to communicate with I/O\n-> Disadvantage is we reduced main memory address space.\n\nIsolated I/O:\n-> I/O devices have a separate address space.\n-> Special I/O instructions are needed to access the I/O address space. eg: IN and OUT - to access I/O ports.\n-> Though the address space is different, a cpu-generated address is a valid memory address and i/o address.\n-> So, we need two address decoders but they will have same address lines connected to them.\n-> To do this, the control unit generates an active low(to enable memory address decoder) when it is executing\n    instructions like load, store etc.. and generates an active high( to enable I/O address decoder ) when instructions like IN and OUT are used.\n-> Main Memory and I/O devices will have same data lines.\n\n\n-> All PROCESSORS support Memory Mapped I/O. Same cannot be said for Isolated I/O.\n-> It is possible to use Memory Mapped I/O and Isolated I/O at same time.\n-> MMIO is faster than Isolated I/O (outdated). Reasons:\n    - To Operate with I/O, Multiple instructions are need as IN and OUT only support moving data from and to registers.\n      Whereas, MMIO can directly manipulate contents on I/O registers. Advance instructions allow to copy I/O reg data directly into ram in a single macro-cycle.\n    - Pipelining works exactly the same for MMIO. For Isolated I/O, since they talk to a completely separate address space, the hardware cannot easily optimise them.\n      For IN instruction to execute, pipeline often has to halt and wait till it completes.\n    - executing IN and OUT requires the kernel mode. transferring data includes copying to kernel buffer and then to user address space.\n      In MMIO, OS utilizes the Memory Management Unit to pull off \"Direct User-space Hardware access\". Kernel allocates specific Virtual memory page in your application's private address space and \n      configures the MMU page tables so that this specific virtual page maps directly to the physical address range of high-speed I/O devices. The application can now directly read and write to the hardware device\n      using stanadrd pointers in C or C__, completely bypassing the OS kernel.\n\n\nIn Modern Computer Systems, The CPU silicon will have one set of data, control and address lines each. But as they creep out onto mother board, they are split into several sets each serving a specific device/purpose.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "I/O Port:\n-> It is an address used by the CPU to communicate with I/O Device\n-> Each register inside the I/O controller has an address. So I/O Port is an address of one of the registers\n\nThere are two ways the I/O devices are connected to System Bus. These also determine how the I/O devices are addressed.\n1) Memory Mapped I/O\n2) Isolated I/O or I/O mapped I/O\n\nMemory Mapped I/O:\n-> I/O devices use the same address space as the main memory\n-> Each device is assigned one or more memory addresses and CPU communicates with the device by reading from or writing to those memory addresses.\n-> The advantage of this is, we can use same instructions we use for memory with I/O devices as well.\n-> The address decoder is hardwired to I/O devices to access them.\n-> There is no need to define new instructions to communicate with I/O\n-> Disadvantage is we reduced main memory address space.\n\nIsolated I/O:\n-> I/O devices have a separate address space.\n-> Special I/O instructions are needed to access the I/O address space. eg: IN and OUT - to access I/O ports.\n-> Though the address space is different, a cpu-generated address is a valid memory address and i/o address.\n-> So, we need two address decoders but they will have same address lines connected to them.\n-> To do this, the control unit generates an active low(to enable memory address decoder) when it is executing\n    instructions like load, store etc.. and generates an active high( to enable I/O address decoder ) when instructions like IN and OUT are used.\n-> Main Memory and I/O devices will have same data lines.\n\n\n-> All PROCESSORS support Memory Mapped I/O. Same cannot be said for Isolated I/O.\n-> It is possible to use Memory Mapped I/O and Isolated I/O at same time.\n-> MMIO is faster than Isolated I/O (outdated). Reasons:\n    - To Operate with I/O, Multiple instructions are need as IN and OUT only support moving data from and to registers.\n      Whereas, MMIO can directly manipulate contents on I/O registers. Advance instructions allow to copy I/O reg data directly into ram in a single macro-cycle.\n    - Pipelining works exactly the same for MMIO. For Isolated I/O, since they talk to a completely separate address space, the hardware cannot easily optimise them.\n      For IN instruction to execute, pipeline often has to halt and wait till it completes.\n    - executing IN and OUT requires the kernel mode. transferring data includes copying to kernel buffer and then to user address space.\n      In MMIO, OS utilizes the Memory Management Unit to pull off \"Direct User-space Hardware access\". Kernel allocates specific Virtual memory page in your application's private address space and \n      configures the MMU page tables so that this specific virtual page maps directly to the physical address range of high-speed I/O devices. The application can now directly read and write to the hardware device\n      using stanadrd pointers in C or C__, completely bypassing the OS kernel.\n\n\nIn Modern Computer Systems, The CPU silicon will have one set of data, control and address lines each. But as they creep out onto mother board, they are split into several sets each serving a specific device/purpose.",
